WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.320
And then trick number three is called tools.

00:04.320 --> 00:10.880
And of course this is a really exciting one and it really took off in a massive way last year and

00:10.880 --> 00:19.600
a bit the year before as well. Tools is about realizing that when we have LLM's generate tokens,

00:19.600 --> 00:29.280
the most likely output, we could use those tokens not just to generate just a text response but also to decide whether or not the LLM

00:29.280 --> 00:36.399
would like to carry out some actions that it's been told it could do. So in the prompt in the input

00:36.399 --> 00:41.679
that you send the LLM you can say hey I'd like you to answer my question but by the way

00:41.679 --> 00:53.000
you can also respond with some special tokens to say I would like to search the internet," or "I would like to use a calculator," or "I would like to run some Python code," and then tell me what.

00:53.000 --> 01:00.000
And if you respond with that, then I will do that action for you, and then I will call you a second time with the results.

01:00.000 --> 01:11.280
And that little clever trick, which is called "using tools," it allows us to use the LLM, not just to generate a text output, but actually to decide to do something.

01:11.280 --> 01:16.739
We call it running a tool and then we will run the tool and provide it back with the response.

01:16.739 --> 01:21.719
But you always have to stay grounded in the reality that the LLM itself

01:21.719 --> 01:26.280
isn't going off and searching the Internet or running Python code.

01:26.280 --> 01:31.120
And LLM is just a statistical engine that generates tokens.

01:31.120 --> 01:34.319
But we are interpreting those tokens.

01:34.319 --> 01:36.719
It's generating tokens that said something like,

01:36.719 --> 01:38.640
I would like to run a calculator.

01:38.640 --> 01:40.120
And we're interpreting that output,

01:40.120 --> 01:41.799
and we're running the calculator.

01:41.799 --> 01:43.519
So it's always our code,

01:43.519 --> 01:46.159
it's always software that ends up calling a tool.

01:46.159 --> 01:49.519
We're using the LLM just to generate the tokens

01:49.519 --> 01:51.760
to say what it wants to do next.

01:51.760 --> 01:56.959
And that whole process is called calling tools and it's really clever.

01:56.959 --> 02:01.439
All right, I just want to make this super real for you just just to be very clear.

02:01.439 --> 02:06.719
Here is something hopefully familiar to you. This is Jout GPT and I'm going to paste in

02:06.719 --> 02:16.000
a question I have for it. Okay, before the question, I want to I want to prefix it to use Python code to respond to the next question.

02:16.000 --> 02:19.000
Just reply, Python colon,

02:19.000 --> 02:22.000
and then a Python expression here.

02:22.000 --> 02:27.000
And then my question, what is the square root of pi,

02:27.000 --> 02:30.000
a question which perhaps isn't going to be very easy

02:30.000 --> 02:32.000
for a language model to respond to,

02:32.000 --> 02:35.000
but I'm telling it that it can do something special.

02:35.000 --> 02:40.319
It can reply this way if it wants to just execute Python code. So I'm going to run that

02:40.319 --> 02:48.080
and we'll see how it responds. And this is how it responds. And you can see that it's done something,

02:48.080 --> 02:51.599
it's done something reasonably advanced because it's doing all in one line, including imports,

02:51.599 --> 03:03.000
which I assume will work. But it's actually decided instead of responding with a square root of pi, it's responding with this Python line of code right here,

03:03.000 --> 03:05.000
quite a clever Python line of code.

03:05.000 --> 03:11.000
And so you can see that basically it's using a tool.

03:11.000 --> 03:15.000
We've equipped chat GBT with this tool that we've just used language for.

03:15.000 --> 03:18.000
And when we've asked it this question, it hasn't given us the answer,

03:18.000 --> 03:21.000
it hasn't just told us the square root of pi.

03:21.000 --> 03:23.560
But rather, it's said that it wants to execute

03:23.560 --> 03:25.360
some Python code.

03:25.360 --> 03:28.599
And this is like a super clear example

03:28.599 --> 03:32.039
of what it means to have an LLM use a tool.

03:32.039 --> 03:35.159
It's just about giving it a clever input

03:35.159 --> 03:37.759
and then interpreting the output.

03:37.759 --> 03:40.240
That is what tool calling is all about.

03:40.240 --> 03:43.879
And the fourth trick is something that's so simple,

03:43.879 --> 03:45.400
it's super elegant.

03:45.400 --> 03:50.800
It's basically saying what could be better than calling an LLM with an input and getting some output?

03:50.800 --> 03:54.400
Or what could be better is calling it multiple times.

03:54.400 --> 04:01.199
In fact, in a loop, we can call an LLM and then when it's produced its output,

04:01.199 --> 04:04.300
we can ask if it's finished yet and if not, we just call it again.

04:04.300 --> 04:07.199
And then again, and then again, in just call it again and then again and then again in just a loop

04:07.199 --> 04:14.319
repeating until a goal is met. And that simple idea of calling an LLM multiple times is something

04:14.319 --> 04:19.600
which turns out to allow us to achieve much bigger goals than just calling an LLM once with

04:19.600 --> 04:25.680
an input and getting an output. And so that fourth trick of the loop is something which allowed us

04:25.680 --> 04:28.120
to achieve much bigger things.

04:29.120 --> 04:31.199
So those are four tricks. There are lots of other tricks,

04:31.199 --> 04:36.240
but these are the big four tricks that allow us to get from the sort of

04:36.240 --> 04:39.279
beginnings, from the chat GPT of 2022,

04:39.560 --> 04:42.800
to some of the high performance products today,

04:43.000 --> 04:45.879
like cursor agent and cloud code. Okay.

04:46.160 --> 04:49.639
Now it's time for me to define an AI agent for you.

